<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/arikesari-maravarman"><span class="enc-name">Arikesari Maravarman</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Arikesari Maravarman was a king of the early medieval Pandya dynasty of Madurai in the later 7th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/arikesari-parakrama-pandya"><span class="enc-name">Arikesari Parakrama Pandya</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Arikesari Parakrama Pandya was a ruler of the later Pandya line settled at Tenkasi in the Tirunelveli region.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/arima-toyouji"><span class="enc-name">Arima Toyouji</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Arima Toyouji was a Japanese daimyo who fought for Tokugawa Ieyasu at Sekigahara in 1600.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/arima-yoriyuki"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/arima-yoriyuki.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Arima Yoriyuki</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Arima Yoriyuki was daimyo of the Kurume Domain in Kyushu and a noted mathematician of the Japanese wasan tradition.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/arinna"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/arinna.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Arinna</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Arinna was a Hittite holy city in central Anatolia, a short distance from the capital Hattusa, whose exact site has not been identified.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ariobarzanes-i-of-cappadocia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ariobarzanes-i-of-cappadocia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ariobarzanes I of Cappadocia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ariobarzanes I Philoromaios was king of Cappadocia from about 95 to 63 BCE, chosen with Roman approval after the Ariarathid line ended.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/arion-of-methymna"><span class="enc-name">Arion of Methymna</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Arion of Methymna was a Greek lyric poet and lyre-player from Methymna on Lesbos, active in the late 7th century BCE, chiefly at the court of the tyrant Periander of…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ariovistus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ariovistus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ariovistus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ariovistus was a Germanic leader of the Suebi who crossed the Rhine into Gaul around 70 BCE as an ally of the Sequani and Arverni against the Aedui.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ariq-boke.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ariq Böke</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ariq Böke was a grandson of Genghis Khan and the youngest brother of Möngke and Kublai.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristagoras-of-cyme"><span class="enc-name">Aristagoras of Cyme</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aristagoras of Cyme was a tyrant of the Aeolian city of Cyme under Persian rule.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristagoras-of-miletus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aristagoras-of-miletus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aristagoras of Miletus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aristagoras was tyrant of Miletus, ruling in place of his father-in-law Histiaeus under Persian authority.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristarchus-of-samos"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aristarchus-of-samos.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aristarchus of Samos</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aristarchus of Samos was a Greek astronomer and mathematician of the third century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristeidis-stergiadis"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aristeidis-stergiadis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aristeidis Stergiadis</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aristeidis Stergiadis (1861–1949) was a Greek lawyer and administrator, appointed by Eleftherios Venizelos as High Commissioner of the Smyrna zone from 1919 to 1922.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristides"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aristides.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aristides</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aristides, called the Just, was an Athenian statesman and general.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristion"><span class="enc-name">Aristion</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aristion was an Athenian philosopher who seized power as tyrant of Athens and brought the city to the side of Mithridates VI of Pontus against Rome in the First…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristippus-of-cyrene"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aristippus-of-cyrene.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aristippus of Cyrene</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aristippus of Cyrene was a Greek philosopher and pupil of Socrates who founded the Cyrenaic school.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristobulus-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aristobulus-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aristobulus I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aristobulus I was a ruler of the Hasmonean dynasty of Judaea, the eldest son of John Hyrcanus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristobulus-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aristobulus-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aristobulus II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aristobulus II was Hasmonean king and high priest of Judaea from 67 to 63 BCE, a younger son of Alexander Jannaeus and Salome Alexandra.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristobulus-of-chalcis"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aristobulus-of-chalcis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aristobulus of Chalcis</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aristobulus of Chalcis was a Herodian prince, a great-grandson of Herod the Great and son of Herod of Chalcis.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristocratic-republic"><span class="enc-name">Aristocratic Republic</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Aristocratic Republic is the name given by the historian Jorge Basadre to the period of Peruvian history from 1895 to 1919, when governments dominated by the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristocyprus"><span class="enc-name">Aristocyprus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aristocyprus was king of Soloi in Cyprus in the early 5th century BCE, son of Philocyprus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristodemus-of-cumae"><span class="enc-name">Aristodemus of Cumae</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aristodemus, called Malakos, was tyrant of Cumae in Campania in the late 6th and early 5th centuries BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristomenes"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aristomenes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aristomenes</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aristomenes is the legendary hero of the Messenians, said to have led their revolt against Sparta in the Second Messenian War in the 7th century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristonicus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aristonicus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aristonicus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aristonicus was a pretender to the throne of Pergamon who claimed to be a son of Eumenes II and took the royal name Eumenes III.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristophanes"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aristophanes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aristophanes</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aristophanes was an Athenian comic playwright, the chief representative of Old Comedy, whose eleven surviving plays include The Clouds, The Birds, Lysistrata and The…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristophanes-of-byzantium"><span class="enc-name">Aristophanes of Byzantium</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aristophanes of Byzantium was a Greek scholar and grammarian who became head of the Library of Alexandria around 195 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristotele-fioravanti"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aristotele-fioravanti.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aristotele Fioravanti</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aristotele Fioravanti was an Italian architect and engineer from Bologna who worked for Ivan III of Moscow from 1475.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristotle"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aristotle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aristotle</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aristotle was a Greek philosopher of the fourth century BCE whose works covered logic, ethics, politics and natural science.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/arita"><span class="enc-name">Arita</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Arita is a town in Saga Prefecture on the island of Kyushu, Japan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/arius"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/arius.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Arius</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Arius was a Christian priest of Alexandria whose teaching that the Son was created by God the Father, and subordinate to him, caused a long controversy in the church.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/arizona"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/arizona.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Arizona</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Arizona is a state in the south-western United States, with its capital at Phoenix.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/arizpe"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/arizpe.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Arizpe</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Arizpe is a town in northern Sonora, Mexico, on the Sonora River, founded as a Jesuit mission in 1646.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/arjona"><span class="enc-name">Arjona</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Arjona is a town in the province of Jaén in Andalusia, southern Spain.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/arjun-pal"><span class="enc-name">Arjun Pal</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Arjun Pal was a Jadaun Rajput chief who founded the town of Karauli in eastern Rajasthan in 1348.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/arjuna"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/arjuna.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Arjuna</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Arjuna is a hero of the Indian epic Mahabharata, the third of the five Pandava brothers and a peerless archer.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/arjunawiwaha"><span class="enc-name">Arjunawiwaha</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Arjunawiwaha, &quot;Arjuna's Wedding&quot;, is an Old Javanese poem (kakawin) written around 1030 by Mpu Kanwa at the court of King Airlangga in East Java.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ark-of-the-covenant"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ark-of-the-covenant.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ark of the Covenant</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ark of the Covenant is, in the Hebrew Bible, a gilded wooden chest holding the tablets of the Law given to Moses.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/arkansas"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/arkansas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Arkansas</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Arkansas is a state of the south-central United States, west of the Mississippi River, with its capital at Little Rock.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/arkhangelsk"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/arkhangelsk.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Arkhangelsk</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Arkhangelsk is a city and port on the Northern Dvina near its mouth on the White Sea, in northern Russia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/arkona"><span class="enc-name">Arkona</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Arkona is a chalk cape on the northern tip of the Baltic island of Rügen in Germany, site of a fortified temple of the Slavic Rani.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/arlanzon-river"><span class="enc-name">Arlanzón</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Arlanzón is a river of northern Spain in the province of Burgos.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/arles"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/arles.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Arles</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Arles is a city on the Rhône in Provence, southern France, near the head of the river's delta.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/arlon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/arlon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Arlon</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Arlon is a town in south-eastern Belgium, the capital of the province of Luxembourg, close to the border with the Grand Duchy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/arma-people"><span class="enc-name">Arma people</span><span